# @starkware-libs/starknet-privacy-sdk lives on GitHub Packages, not public npm, and GitHub
# Packages requires auth even for public packages.
#
# Set GH_PACKAGES_TOKEN (a PAT with read:packages) wherever the install runs:
#   local   — export it from ~/.zshrc, or use direnv
#   deploys — add it to the platform's environment variables (Vercel/Netlify/Docker/CI)
#
# Not named GITHUB_TOKEN on purpose: GitHub Actions injects a variable by that name
# automatically, and it is scoped to this repository only — it cannot read another
# organisation's packages, so the collision would fail in a confusing way.
#
# Do not put a literal token here; this file is committed. Do not put one in ~/.npmrc either
# while this line exists — an auth line here always wins, and with the variable unset bun sends
# the literal string "${GH_PACKAGES_TOKEN}" instead of falling back.
#
# This must stay at the repo root. bun resolves .npmrc from the directory the install runs in,
# so a workspace install from the root ignores web/.npmrc and silently falls back to
# registry.npmjs.org, which 404s and reads like a missing package.
@starkware-libs:registry=https://npm.pkg.github.com
//npm.pkg.github.com/:_authToken=${GH_PACKAGES_TOKEN}
